  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 235 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nWhile models continue to differ on the details, specifically on the ingestion\nof extra-tropical system Polo, and its movement northward across the southern\nRockies into southeast Colorado, there is enough agreement in ensemble data\nto support a high impact system affecting southern Colorado through Thursday.\n\nCurrent forecast supports moist south to southwest flow aloft across the \nregion Tuesday night, with increasing south to southeast flow through the \nday Wednesday, as a strengthening upper low moves into northwest New Mexico.\nThis will bring widespread light to moderate rain across southern Colorado\nTuesday night with moderate to heavy deformation banded precipitation \nspreading across south central and southeast Colorado through the day \nWednesday and Wednesday night, as the upper low continues across northern \nNew Mexico. Widespread precipitation then winds down northwest to southeast \nthrough the day Thursday, as the deformation band lifts north and east into \nthe Central High Plains. The movement of this system, and its developing \nsurface low reflection, will also develop gusty northerly gradient winds of \n20 to 40 mph, with locally stronger gusts across eastern Colorado later \nWednesday afternoon and Wednesday night, which could bring enhanced \norographic precipitation to the Palmer Divide and Pikes Peak region, as well \nas the southern Sangres and Raton Mesa.      \n \nWith that said, we have expanded flash flood watches in coverage and time\nacross most of south central and southeast Colorado, with current forecast \nof 2 to 4 inches of liquid qpf, greatest across the southeast mountains \nand adjacent plains over the 3 day period. For the flash flooding threat, the \nlack of instability and its associated enhancement on precipitation rates,\nis a concern, however, the shear amount of moisture (PWATs progged up to 250 \npercent of normal), dynamics, upslope and warm rain processes look to support\nlocally heavy rain and rates up to inch or more inch an hour at times. This \ncombined, with the long duration and expected increasing saturated soils, \nespecially across the southeast plains, which have already seen 2 to 4 inches\nof rain this month, will support flash flooding, along with quickly rising \nflows in streams and rivers across the area. The Aspen Acres fire area will \nalso be susceptible to flash flooding and mud flows, with the potential for \nfallen trees, as the gusty northerly winds develop later Wednesday. Latest \nmodel data also supports several inches of higher elevation (at or above \n12,000 feet) through Thursday. \n\nWe will continue to monitor the track of this developing storm system, \nwith potentially higher impacts, if it tracks more north than currently\nforecast, as well as lesser possible impacts, if it goes further south.\nEither way, people should remain weather aware through Thursday, and have \nmultiple ways to receive weather warnings, especially those in flood prone \nareas.  \n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, warm and drier air within more westerly flow \nworks back into the region, as upper level ridging builds back across the \nGreat Basin and into the Rockies. This leads to a slow warming and drying \ntrend, as there may be enough residual moisture to support isolated afternoon \nconvection over the higher terrain.",
